    {
      "id": "67",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 67,
        "body_ja": null,
        "body": "<p>In order to say that something 'was' a certain way in Japanese, you will need to use <span data-gp-id='40'>だった</span> (casual) or <span data-gp-id='40'>でした</span> (polite) attached to the end of a noun or <span data-gp-id='28'>な-Adjective</span>. This is simply a combination of two <span data-term='助動詞・じょどうし'>auxiliary verbs</span>, <span data-gp-id='1'>だ</span> (or <span data-gp-id='2'>です</span> when polite) with た. For <span data-gp-id='40'>だった</span>, we will need to add った to the base form of <span data-gp-id='1'>だ</span>. For <span data-gp-id='2'>です</span>, す will change to し before adding た.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154956'>\r\n<li data-study-question='154957'></ul>\r\n\r\n<section class='caution'><h4>Caution</h4>\r\n<p>The past form of <span data-gp-id='24'>い-Adjectives</span> cannot be linked with <span data-gp-id='40'>だった</span> or <span data-gp-id='40'>でした</span>, as they have their own set of conjugation rules.</p></section>"
      }
    },
